# Phase 3 Step 3.2: pin line endings for files where the lock-integrity CI
# job compares committed bytes against newly regenerated bytes.
#
# pip-compile writes lock files using the host OS line ending (CRLF on Windows,
# LF on Linux). Without this attribute, git's autocrlf could rewrite line
# endings during checkout, causing tools/diff_locks.py to report spurious
# whole-file diffs. We pin requirements*.lock to LF so the source-of-truth
# representation in the repo is OS-independent. The lock-integrity job
# regenerates with the same LF convention on Windows via Git Bash's pip-compile.
requirements*.lock text eol=lf

# Phase 5 Step 5.a 完結 K=2 r4 fix #1: pin line endings for all build input
# files. The Phase 5 converter (tools/build_skill.py, to be implemented in 5.b)
# computes build_input_digest over byte-level content of these files. Without
# LF pinning, git's autocrlf on Windows could rewrite line endings during
# checkout, causing the digest to become environment-dependent (CRLF on
# Windows, LF on Linux). This breaks golden fixture byte equality and
# defeats the deterministic build contract documented in
# development/docs/skill-converter/acceptance-matrix.md "deterministic build 契約".
#
# We pin both authored sources under skills/source/ and the docs that the
# converter reads (development/docs/skill-converter/agent-*.md front matter).
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
